{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 46, :comments ["<b>And Jacob said to his brethren: Gather stones.</b> To the brethren of Lavan. And like it (Genesis 47:3): ״ויאמר פרעה לאחיו מה מעשיכם״ — to the brethren of Joseph." "<b>And in the Midrash.</b> 'His brethren' — these are his sons, and he would call them 'brethren' because they were mighty men, like him righteous, like him." "<b>And behold,</b> Jacob lifted a stone for a pillar and commanded Lavan's brethren that they gather stones like it, to be pillars, and he did not say that they should make a heap (gal); but they gathered stones and made a heap (gal). And their intention was toward their faith and their worship, for they worshipped the sun and would inquire on behalf of the living to the dead. Therefore Scripture mentions 'heap' (gal) twice, for it could have said ״ויאכלו שם עליו״, but it did so in order to allude to their intention in the name גלגל (galgal), that is, the sphere of the sun. And our matter is explained in that name which Lavan called it, יגר שהדותה — he reckoned it the sphere of the sun, and it was his god that he prepared as a witness. For the change of expression indicates a novel matter without doubt. And perhaps the teraphim received power from the astrological forces of the sun, and therefore Lavan said ״הגל הזה עד ביני וביניך״ — an expression that applies to both senses. And he mentioned the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He, in counterpart to Jacob's faith." "<b>And He mentioned</b> in this the two attributes by which the world was created: the attribute of justice and the attribute of mercy. He first mentioned the attribute of justice in the allusion — this is what is said: ״והיה לעד ביני ובינך״. And the word והיה are the letters of the special Name (השם המיוחד), and this is what Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: ״והיה לעד״ — this is the Holy One, blessed be He. And He further mentioned explicitly ״יצף HaShem ביני ובינך״, and afterward He mentioned the attribute of justice and said ״אלהים עד ביני ובינך״. And from this he said: ״אלהי אברהם ואלהי נחור ישפטו בינינו״ — 'the god of Abraham' is the attribute of mercy and the attribute of justice which He mentioned, and 'the god of Nahor' is the sphere of the sun, which is his god that he alluded to in the name גל. And this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, said: אלהי אברהם — holy; ואלהי נחור — profane." "<b>And know</b> that everything that is called 'every heaven and heaven' 'גלגל' (galgal, sphere) — it would be fitting to call it גל (gal), which is an expression of height. And the intent of this is the height of the spheres, in the manner of what is written (Job 11:8): ״גבהי שמים מה תפעל״; but the word is doubled to indicate the encompassing of their circuit and their motion, and further to indicate that their actions and forces change one opposite the other."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:57.338954992Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}